## Design the codebase for agent locality and small blast radius
Organize by domain/business capability first (e.g., billing/, identity/), then vertical slices for individual use cases (changePassword/, refundPayment/) rather than pure technical layers (controllers/, services/, utils/).
Prefer cohesive, self-contained modules with strict boundaries so an agent can load a narrow working set, make a change, and verify it without exploring the whole system.
Enforce low coupling and high coherence (Dependency Inversion with consumer-defined interfaces). Treat modules as potentially disposable/regenerable when boundaries are clear.
Use bounded contexts (DDD-style) as containment strategy. Keep shared technical concerns inside domain boundaries where possible.
Goal: an unfamiliar agent should find the right context, change one bounded area, and run a narrow verification loop without guessing or broad side effects.

## Prefer small, reviewable, reversible changes and isolation
Default to minimum-diff, high-locality edits. Avoid premature abstractions (e.g., “three-callers rule” before extracting helpers).

## Continuous maintenance and cleanup loops
Address dead/unused code with removal, stale feature flags/experiments, flaky tests, CI cost reduction, documentation drift as it occurs or is create due to works.

## Make architecture and standards executable and discoverable
Make architecture and standards executable and discoverable
Enforce modularity, dependency direction, complexity budgets, and standards via CI (structural rules, smell detection, test-impact analysis).
Keep navigability high: clear entry points, consistent naming, searchable structure so agents (and humans) orient quickly.

